**Lemma 3.3 (exact acquired-word correspondence and actual positivity).** The valid first-$p$ histories are precisely

$$
h(w)=\operatorname{enc}(w)\,\beta\alpha\,\beta\beta\alpha\alpha,
\qquad w\in(\Gamma\cup\{I\})^*.
\tag{3.3}
$$

They have seed 1, marks 100 and the same original record configuration $c_*$; their acquired length is $L|w|+6$, and $A(h)=B(h)=2t|w|+3$. Moreover $A_{h(w)}=p_A(w)$. All other original histories, with every seed and record, remain in the actual domain and have $A_h=0$ at a first-$p$ cut if invalid. All subsequent $p$ cuts have $A_h=0$.

**Proof.** A seed rejection is exactly an equal pair in the original seed parser. The finite checker partitions them into length-$2t$ token blocks. Its valid accepted seed must occur at a block boundary, and the injective codebook then recovers a unique $w$. Its required unequal pair is $\beta\alpha$. After that seed, $\beta\beta$ completes mark 1 in segment 1, $\alpha$ completes mark 0 in segment 2 and $\alpha$ completes mark 0 in segment 3. A strict suffix check admits no hidden extra return. Conversely every (3.3) follows exactly those original transitions. The code blocks do not alter seed or tree records; original $C_0$ has no erased-length counter. Hence $c_*$ is independent of $w$. Balanced counts in (3.2) plus the three alphas and three betas of the suffix give the stated counts and length.

Conditioning on an actual acquired word does not condition on private random updates: given $K$, the source's word probability depends only on $K$ and its letter counts, not on the independent program bits. Thus the joint conditional distribution of $K$ and the sampled label factors into its actual posterior and $\delta_{q_0}M_w$. This proves the claimed $A_h$, without storing or evaluating that probability row. Every finite history (3.3) has probability $\sum_k\mu(k)[r_k(1-r_k)]^{2t|w|+3}>0$. Invalidity neither disables a Read nor modifies an original event, so it removes no other actual history. Discarding the private label on the first fourth Read ensures $A_h=0$ on every return. ∎

